{
  "requirement": "Overrides the SDK’s OpenAI model, endpoint, credentials, or inference options. Omitted settings use the defaults in the agent configuration contract.\n\n```rust,ignore\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub struct OpenAiConfig {\n    pub model: Option<String>,\n    pub endpoint: Option<String>,\n    pub api_key_env: Option<String>,\n    pub api_key: Option<SecretString>,\n    pub options: BTreeMap<String, OptionValue>,\n}\n\nimpl OpenAiConfig {\n    pub fn new() -> Self;\n    pub fn model(self, model: String) -> Self;\n    pub fn endpoint(self, endpoint: String) -> Self;\n    pub fn api_key_env(self, api_key_env: String) -> Self;\n    pub fn api_key(self, api_key: SecretString) -> Self;\n    pub fn option(self, key: String, value: OptionValue) -> Self;\n}\n```\n\nTrait implementations\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl Clone for OpenAiConfig;\nimpl Debug for OpenAiConfig;\nimpl Default for OpenAiConfig;\nimpl<'de> Deserialize<'de> for OpenAiConfig;\nimpl PartialEq for OpenAiConfig;\nimpl Serialize for OpenAiConfig;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "Obligations and the tests that cover them:\n\n- **Struct shape and builders.** `new` and the builders `model`, `endpoint`, `api_key_env`, `api_key` and `option` must set only the requested fields. `config.rs#mod[tests]/fn[backend_builders_set_only_the_requested_fields]` checks that `OpenAiConfig::new()` serializes to `{}`. It then applies every builder and asserts the exact JSON. That JSON includes `api_key`, and a repeated `option` key is overwritten (`a` ends as 2).\n- **Serialize, Deserialize and PartialEq.** `fn[all_backends_preserve_nested_settings_and_credentials]` round-trips an OpenAI config with nested options through JSON and compares the results. `fn[openai_inference_options_only_fill_missing_defaults]` deserializes `{\"backend\":\"openai\"}` and compares it with `OpenAiConfig::new()`.\n- **Defaults for omitted settings.** `fn[option_defaults_and_credential_resolution_are_deterministic]` asserts the default model (`gpt-6.1-sol`), the default endpoint, and the default options (`max_output_tokens` 16384, `reasoning.effort` low) for `OpenAiConfig::default()`. `fn[openai_inference_options_only_fill_missing_defaults]` covers the user-override cases. A supplied `max_output_tokens` wins. A supplied `reasoning` map gets `effort` filled in only when it is missing. A supplied `effort` is kept.\n- **Credentials.** The same test covers a missing environment variable (the error names the variable), an explicit `api_key` taking precedence, and an empty or missing value being an error. `fn[openai_uses_the_default_credential_variable]` re-runs itself in a child process with `OPENAI_API_KEY` set and asserts the default variable is used.\n- **Overrides take effect end to end.** `configured.rs#mod[tests]/fn[supplied_openai_backend_runs_independent_calls_against_a_local_transport]` supplies a custom model, endpoint and key. It asserts the requests carry model `local-test`, and that the key and backend settings do not appear in the saved evidence.\n- **Public API.** `tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` checks that the exported inventory matches the book.\n\nThe `Clone`, `Debug` and `Default` impls and the `#[non_exhaustive]` attribute come from a plain `#[derive(Clone, Debug, Default, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize)]` plus the attribute on `struct[OpenAiConfig]`. They have no conditional logic or transformation of their own. `Default` and `Debug` are also exercised indirectly. I found no unacknowledged adequacy gap.",
  "references": "1e27ef26f764835e7d12b55c94dc204c3b3fe9b16be8fc68565e50322e5ce3e9",
